    {
      "id": "port-template-view",
      "type": "view",
      "label": "View",
      "position": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 320
      },
      "width": 420,
      "height": 260,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "view": {
          "intent": "node",
          "payload": "node.web.detail"
        },
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "port-template",
          "nodeId": "port-template-view"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "html",
          "value": "<div style='box-sizing:border-box;width:100%;height:100%;padding:24px;background:#ffffff;font-family:system-ui;color:#0f172a'><h1 style='margin:0 0 12px;font:800 22px/1.2 system-ui'>Port's own surfaces</h1><p style='margin:0;max-width:60ch;font:400 15px/1.6 system-ui;color:#334155'>A Port offers its own surfaces relationship, the same mechanism a Declaration uses -- a graph wired to this Port enters here, not through the Declaration's surface.</p></div>"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "port-template-content",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Content",
      "position": {
        "x": 480,
        "y": 320
      },
      "width": 460,
      "height": 320,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "port-template",
          "nodeId": "port-template-content"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "html",
          "value": "<div style='box-sizing:border-box;width:100%;height:100%;padding:28px;background:#ffffff;font-family:system-ui;color:#0f172a;overflow:auto'><h1 style='margin:0 0 12px;font:800 24px/1.2 system-ui'>Why this Port shows the eye icon</h1><p style='margin:0 0 12px;max-width:62ch;font:400 15px/1.6 system-ui;color:#334155'>Inside the graph editor, this Port's root handle always shows the same Login glyph every primitive's root carries -- that is set structurally, in portDeclarations.js, regardless of this node's own data.</p><p style='margin:0 0 12px;max-width:62ch;font:400 15px/1.6 system-ui;color:#334155'>But once this graph is opened as an instance inside a <em>workspace</em> (another graph's canvas, with this one shown as a tile), an exposed Port gets a second, separate icon: the little capability-handle button drawn on the tile's own boundary, the one you can drag from to spin up a Portal into this graph. That icon comes from a different file entirely -- workspaceCapabilities.js reads <code>portNode.data.presentation.glyph || portNode.data.glyph</code>, an authored override most Port nodes never set. When it's unset, WorkspaceLayer.js falls back to a hardcoded <code>{ kind: 'icon', name: 'Visibility' }</code> for every exposed Port -- the eye icon, standing in for \"this is a way to see into / connect through this graph.\" Bridge gets Cable, Portal gets MeetingRoom, by the same fallback; Port gets Visibility.</p><p style='margin:0;max-width:62ch;font:400 15px/1.6 system-ui;color:#334155'>Set <code>data.glyph</code> on this Port node yourself and the workspace tile will show that instead -- the fallback only fires when nothing more specific is authored.</p></div>"
        }
      }
    },
    {
